<h3>In three lines</h3>
<ul>
    <li>On October 21 (US time) Anthropic opened a research preview of <strong>Claude Duet</strong>, a separate app that brings you work before you ask and keeps going on its own cloud computer while you are away. US Pro and Max subscribers get in through a waitlist.</li>
    <li>Actions fall into <strong>three tiers</strong>. Reading, drafting and preparing happen on their own. Sending, paying, signing and deleting are shown to you first and wait for approval. Trading securities and moving money between accounts <strong>do not happen even if you approve</strong>. The model never directly sees your passwords or sign-in tokens.</li>
    <li>Duet is the third always-on personal agent in six weeks, after Meta Muse (September 8) and OpenAI Dots (September 29). The three differ less in features than in <strong>where the approval gate sits</strong> and <strong>who pays for background work</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<p>Until now Claude answered when asked. Duet reverses the order. Each morning it skims your connected mail and calendar and sends a short brief, then checks again every few hours and messages you only when something needs attention. An agent that finds work and raises it before the user asks is called a <strong>proactive agent</strong>. Anthropic pitches Duet at professionals and small business owners and says it was designed around Claude Opus 5.5. This article pulls together the official announcement, the help center guide and launch-week coverage to diagram how Duet spends a day, what it does alone and what it asks about, and how it differs from its rivals.</p>

<h3>A day with Duet: from morning brief to approval ping</h3>
<p>You talk to Duet in a single ongoing thread. Hand it a task by text or voice and it carries on in the browser of its own virtual machine (VM, a separate computer in the cloud). Your phone or laptop can be off and the work keeps going. You get a notification when a task finishes or needs a human decision.</p>
