<p>The interval of a few hours is deliberate. Anthropic says constant monitoring would use up plan limits too fast. Duet's background checks and morning brief come out of the five-hour and weekly limits at the same rate as chat. Bigger jobs such as a financial model or a deck take more model turns and more background work, so they cost more. Finished work lands as editable files in the <strong>Make</strong> tab. Active work and anything that needs your input sit in the <strong>Tasks</strong> tab, and only you can mark a task completed.</p>
<p>Duet inherits the memory, skills and connected apps of your Claude account from the first message, so there is little to set up. It can also search your Duet chats and your Claude chats outside projects, and you can turn that off under Memory in settings.</p>

<h3>What it does alone, and what it asks about</h3>
<p>The clearest part of Duet's design is the three tiers, sorted by how reversible an action is. In the announcement's words, before it sends a message, makes a payment, signs a document or deletes anything, it "shows you exactly what it's about to do and waits for your approval." And "a few things are off-limits even with your approval."</p>
