<h3>The third always-on agent in six weeks</h3>
<p>Duet did not arrive alone. Meta launched its personal agent Muse on September 8, and OpenAI launched Dots, always-on agents inside ChatGPT, at DevDay on September 29. All three run continuously in the cloud, look for work on their own and call a human before important actions. The details differ.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap cmp">
<table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Claude Duet</th><th>OpenAI Dots</th><th>Meta Muse</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Launch</strong></td><td>Oct 21 · research preview</td><td>Sep 29 · DevDay</td><td>Sep 8 · US launch</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Model</strong></td><td>Claude Opus 5.5</td><td>GPT-6 Astra</td><td>Muse Spark family</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Where it works</strong></td><td>Its own VM and browser</td><td>A cloud computer and browser per dot</td><td>Runs in the background</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Proactive part</strong></td><td>Morning brief, checks every few hours</td><td>"Proactive research" when idle (app access read-only then)</td><td>Reports at milestones toward a goal</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Approval gate</strong></td><td>Fixed by action type: approve send, pay, sign, delete; trading and transfers barred</td><td>User-written Custom Rules: allow, require approval, block</td><td>Comes back when confirmation is needed</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Connections</strong></td><td>6,000+ connectors and plugins</td><td>4,000+ apps</td><td>Mail, calendar, payments and more</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Price and limits</strong></td><td>In Pro and Max; background work counts against limits</td><td>First dot included in Pro and Business Premium; chats with a dot don't count against limits</td><td>Free to start; Power $20, Maximum $100 per month</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>The biggest split is where the approval gate goes. Dots lets users write rules for what to allow and what to ask about. That is flexible, and a badly written rule runs exactly as written. Duet's lines were drawn in advance by Anthropic, by action type. It is less flexible, and there is no way for a user to accidentally allow a transfer. Usage accounting runs the opposite way too. Dots leaves conversations with a dot out of the limits, while Duet charges even the work it does alone in the background. That accounting is why its checks come only every few hours.</p>

<h3>Where it still stumbles</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>It can misjudge urgency.</strong> Anthropic itself writes that it "might misread a thread or miscalibrate what's urgent." Checks are hours apart, so a truly urgent item can wait for the next one.</li>
    <li><strong>You cannot see its browser.</strong> During the preview you cannot watch or take over Duet's cloud browser.</li>
    <li><strong>Personal errands are weaker.</strong> Anthropic says tasks like booking tickets are not as strong as work tasks.</li>
    <li><strong>No calls or texts.</strong> It sends email from connected accounts (after approval) but does not make calls or send texts. It does not see all the projects and files on your computer, and it can read your Claude work but not change it.</li>
    <li><strong>Work it never brought back.</strong> A launch-week outside review described a task that sat in Duet's checklist without Duet raising it again, so by the time the user came back their attention had moved on. For an agent that speaks first, what it leaves unsaid is part of its performance.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How this wiki reads it</h3>
<p>In <a href="#!news-20260820-who-makes-the-call">Who Gets to Make the Call</a>, this wiki covered Claude Tag letting the model decide when to step in. Duet takes that judgment out of a Slack channel and spreads it over a user's whole day. The model decides what is urgent and when to speak. The difference shows in the bill. Claude Tag kept the cost of reading extra context out of the limits, while Duet counts even the time it spends looking around on its own. Duet's "every few hours" is less a technical limit than a pricing design.</p>
<p>On approvals, Duet confirms that article's conclusion: judgment does not disappear, it moves. In Duet it sits in three places: a classifier that screens every action, an approval request on your phone, and a deny list nobody can open. The last one resembles what <a href="#!news-20261014-ledger-write-mcp">Agents That Write to the Ledger</a> found in accounting software's period lock. Some writes are not left to an approval button at all and are closed one layer above it.</p>
<p>This wiki's news candidates are also picked each morning by a scheduled Claude session. The session reads and summarizes, sends a notification, and registers an article only after a reply with item numbers. It is a small version of the same split between what an agent does alone and what waits for approval.</p>

<h3>Fact-check notes</h3>
<p class="scn-sub">The hero image is the share image (the app icon) from Anthropic's Claude Duet announcement, saved to the repository as webp. Features, approval rules, the deny list, credential handling (same design as Claude Managed Agents), the sensitive-data classifier, the auto mode classifier, Link payments, the 6,000-plus connectors and example tasks, shared limits, limitations and use cases follow Anthropic's <a href="https://claude.com/resources/articles/claude-duet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Introducing Claude Duet</a>. The tab layout (Make, Tasks), finishing other work while an approval waits, source citations, the separate password form, chat search and where to turn it off, no Microsoft 365 and limited Slack, no calls or texts, no model choice, the ban on accessing someone else's account and the one-time limit reset on admission (until November 30) follow the Claude help article <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/17631495-get-started-with-claude-duet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">"Get started with Claude Duet"</a>. <strong>The two official sources differ on platforms.</strong> The announcement lists iOS, Android and claude.ai, while the help article only gives iPhone and Android setup steps, so the body keeps both. The launch date (October 21) matches the <a href="https://x.com/claudeai/status/2112984664256241837" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official Claude account on X</a> and multiple reports. The specific description of a proxy fetching credentials and making API calls comes from coverage that unpacks the announcement's "same design as Managed Agents" (<a href="https://www.implicator.ai/anthropic-claude-duet-business-tasks/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Implicator</a> and others). OpenAI Dots (September 29, GPT-6 Astra, cloud computer, read-only proactive research, Custom Rules, 4,000-plus apps, chats not counted against limits) follows <a href="https://siliconangle.com/2026/09/29/openai-launches-dots-always-on-ai-agents-in-chatgpt-with-their-own-cloud-computers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SiliconANGLE</a> and <a href="https://betanews.com/article/openai-dots-agents-chatgpt/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BetaNews</a>. Meta Muse (September 8, US, free to start, Power $20 and Maximum $100) follows <a href="https://about.fb.com/news/2026/09/introducing-muse-personal-ai-agent/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Meta's announcement</a> and <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/08/meta-debuts-its-muse-ai-agent-will-consumers-trust-it/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TechCrunch</a>. Sources disagree on the version of Muse's underlying model, so the table says only "Muse Spark family." <strong>The note that scheduled runs cannot use custom connectors and the "never brought back" case come from coverage summarizing outside reviews; the reviews themselves were not read.</strong> Customers and Anthropic staff quoted in the announcement are not named, except Boris Cherny, who is a public figure. The environment used to write this article could not open some news pages because of its network policy, so the two official sources were read directly and the rest is carried over only where search summaries agree.</p>
